          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What does osteoarthritis guidance describe as core treatment before anything else?",
          "answer": "According to the osteoarthritis guideline summary (guideline-osteoarthritis-management), the core first step is non-drug management, and structured exercise is the main part of it. The guideline says exercise has the strongest and most consistent evidence for knee and hip osteoarthritis, with a better safety profile than many medicines. The program combines muscle strengthening around the joint, range-of-movement work, and aerobic activity. It is best started with supervision, at least at first.\n\nThe general reference on osteoarthritis (condition-osteoarthritis) adds that weight management is part of care where relevant, along with education and pacing.\n\nThe lifestyle note on exercising with osteoarthritis (lifestyle-osteoarthritis-activity) explains what the three parts involve. Strengthening targets the muscles around the affected joint, range-of-movement work keeps the joint moving, and aerobic activity supports general fitness and weight.\n\nThese passages do not cover how to choose a specific exercise plan for your own joint, or how exercise compares with other treatments in detail for your situation. A clinician or physiotherapist can help with that.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
